oldlibc - modules of the libc versions that the original programs were linked with
===================================================================================

The shipped library (/lib/libc.a, sources in ../libc) is not the only libc inside the original programs.
Programs carry the members they were linked with, and some of them come from other revisions. This
directory holds only libc modules that are not the shipped ones:

    <module>/0_<n>.s     version n of the libc module (named like the member of libc.a: printf, doprnt, ...)
    VERSIONS             which program contains which version of a module
    CATALOG.md           which libc members each program contains and how they differ (made by
                         host/tools/libcver.py --catalog)

The sources are assembler (cas syntax) cut out of the original programs by `host/tools/unlink.py`; there is no
C source and none is planned. The version numbers are invented (0_1, 0_2, ...) until we know the real ones;
the same text gets the same number.

Members of the shipped libc.a that an older cc built are NOT here: they are in `../libc/oldcc/<function>/<version>.s`.

How a program uses them: the program has its own directory in `../../cmd` (size, strip, ranlib, cc) with a
list of its libc modules in link order (`<program>.spec` input and `<program>.link` result of unlink.py). Its
Makefile assembles the old modules from here, extracts the modules that are identical with the shipped ones
from `/lib/libc.a` (`ar x`) and links them behind the own objects. No libc is linked. The first program is
`../../cmd/size` (13 old modules: rdwr printf doprnt ffltioc filbuf flsbuf endopen findiop cnvt length malloc
goodmagic swap, and 19 shipped members), `../../cmd/strip` (16 old modules and 24 shipped members),
`../../cmd/ranlib` (24 old modules and 19 shipped members) and `../../cmd/cc` (cc and scc: 9 old modules and 33 shipped
members; these two files are byte-identical with the originals). Modules with the same text in several programs are one
version (for example cnvt, ffltioc, length in cc, scc, size and strip; strcpy in cc, scc and strip); see VERSIONS.

How the modules are cut:
 * a module is a stretch of text that no relative branch (jr, djnz, calr) and no jump table crosses; the
   assembler cannot relocate those. The boundaries and names come from a spec written by hand (call graph,
   sizes of the shipped members, symbol table of libc.a).
 * calls between modules, data and bss addresses are symbols; local labels count from the start of the module
   (`L0042`, `D0010`); jump tables are `.addr` of local labels. Pointers in other data are still numbers.
 * the data belongs to the modules in link order.

Versions found so far (CATALOG.md): the programs ar, getud, putud, tar, dump, dumpdir, restor, copydisk, format,
verify, sync use the shipped libc; cc and scc have one libc part of their own, size, strip and ranlib one each
(strip shares six modules with size). Open: how many libc source revisions and how many older compilers are behind
that. Not covered: the segmented programs (cas, sld, ...), they link slibc.a.

Regenerate the catalogue (file tree of the original system in WEGA):

    WEGA=<tree> python3 host/tools/libcver.py --catalog bin/ar bin/cc bin/scc bin/size usr/bin/strip \
        usr/bin/ranlib bin/getud bin/putud bin/tar etc/dump etc/dumpdir etc/restor bin/copydisk \
        bin/format bin/verify bin/sync > src/lib/oldlibc/CATALOG.md

Cut a program (here size, from src/cmd/size after `make` built its crt0-size.o and size.o):

    WEGA=<tree> python3 host/tools/unlink.py src/cmd/size/size.spec <tree>/bin/size src/lib/oldlibc \
        src/lib/libc/crt/crt0-size.o src/cmd/size/size.o > src/cmd/size/size.link
